```c
void pLayer_hls(block_t block) {
  block_t initial;
  #pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=initial complete
  #pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=block complete
  copyBlock_hls(block, initial);

  for (unsigned char i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
    #pragma HLS UNROLL
    unsigned char temp = 0;
    for (unsigned char j_pair = 0; j_pair < 4; j_pair++) {
      #pragma HLS UNROLL
      const unsigned char indexVal = (4 * (i & 1)) + (3 - j_pair);
      const unsigned char nibble = i >> 1;
      const unsigned char base_bit = 3 - nibble;
      
      const unsigned char byte = initial[indexVal];
      const unsigned char mask_even = 1 << base_bit;
      const unsigned char mask_odd = 1 << (base_bit + 4);
      
      temp |= ((byte & mask_even) ? 1 : 0) << (j_pair*2);
      temp |= ((byte & mask_odd) ? 1 : 0) << (j_pair*2 + 1);
    }
    block[i] = temp;
  }
}

void pLayer(block_t *block) {
  pLayer_hls(*block);
}
```

Key optimizations:
1. **Pair-wise bit processing**: Processes 2 bits (even/odd) per iteration to reduce memory access conflicts
2. **Memory conflict resolution**: Restructured to ensure single read per initial[indexVal] for 2-bit processing
3. **Bitmask pre-calculation**: Explicit calculation of even/odd bit positions using base_bit offset
4. **Parallel mask testing**: Simultaneous evaluation of both even and odd bits from the same byte
5. **Aggressive array partitioning**: Complete partitioning of both block arrays for maximum parallel access
6. **Constant propagation**: Marked all intermediate calculations as constants for optimal synthesis

This version specifically targets the memory access pattern bottleneck identified in previous implementations. By processing bit pairs from the same source byte, we eliminate read conflicts while maintaining full parallelism through complete loop unrolling.
